{{if .Slng.Any}}
## SLNG Context Router

This agent's thinking goes through the SLNG Context Router. It caches the turns
this agent has already answered and serves them without calling the model, so a
repeated turn comes back in roughly a tenth of the time.

Every think request carries two identity headers:

| Header | Value |
|---|---|
| `X-Slng-Agent-Id` | the id from the package, one value for this whole project. It scopes the cache. |
| `X-Slng-Session-Id` | one uuid per call, created where the call begins. It groups this call's requests and scopes nothing. |

The model configuration travels inline in the request body, which means **this
agent sends the upstream provider's credentials to SLNG on every think request**.
Each one is read from the environment at startup and no file in this project holds
a value, but the credentials do leave for SLNG. Every name is in the startup check
above, so a missing one stops the agent at boot rather than mid-call.

The system prompt goes to the router with its `{{"{{"}}name{{"}}"}}` placeholders
intact, and this call's values travel beside it. That is what lets a personalised
prompt still repeat. A value over 4000 characters is truncated with a warning on
stderr and the call continues: an over-long variable never ends a call.
{{if .Slng.Vertex}}
The Vertex credential variable may hold the key JSON, that JSON base64 encoded, or
a path to the key file. The agent decides which at startup, in that order, and
fails at boot naming all three shapes if it is none of them. To produce the base64
form:

```sh
base64 < service-account.json | tr -d '\n'
```
{{end}}
### Reading the response

Three response headers say which path answered. They are the only way to check
this is working:

| Header | Values | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `x-slng-response-source` | `llm`, `cache` | which path answered |
| `x-slng-cache-layer` | `l1_exact`, `l2_exact`, absent on the model path | which cache layer |
| `x-slng-model` | a model id, present only on the model path | which model answered |
| `x-slng-request-id` | always present, errors included | quote this to support |

**The first turn of every call takes the model path.** There is no preceding turn
to have cached. A conversation with no repeated turn in it will show no cache hits
at all, and that is correct.

**Not every repeated turn caches.** The router judges which turns are repeatable,
so a repeat served by the model is expected rather than a fault.

Tool turns always take the model path, both the request turn and the result turn.
Streamed responses carry no usage on either path, so token savings cannot be read
off the stream.

### When it fails

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| every tool turn returns 400, `Function tools with reasoning_effort are not supported` | the reasoning models in the GPT-5 family reject function tools on chat completions without it | set `params.reasoning_effort: "none"` on the think binding |
| every think request returns 400, `cannot be resolved: no org config found` | the request reached the router without its inline configuration | this agent always sends it, so the body was stripped or malformed in transit |
| 400 starting `invalid slng_config:` | a malformed inline configuration | the message names the failing field; credentials are never echoed back |
| 400 `inline slng_config is not enabled` | inline configuration is off for the organisation | ask your SLNG contact to enable it |
| every think request returns 401 | the SLNG key is wrong or rotated | replace `SLNG_API_KEY` |
| an upstream 401 or quota error inside a 400 body | the upstream credential is wrong or out of credit | replace the value behind that upstream's variable |
| the agent refuses to start naming an environment variable | a declared credential has no value | set it. The startup check exists so this does not happen mid-call |
| the agent refuses to start naming three accepted shapes | a Vertex credential is not JSON, not base64 of JSON, and not a readable file path | fix the value |
| 422 `missing_template_variables` | a literal `{{"{{"}}` in the prompt that is not a placeholder | remove or rephrase it: the router reads any `{{"{{"}}` as a placeholder |
| answers stay slow across deploys | the agent id changed between them | keep one stable id and bump it deliberately |

{{end}}## Deploy

Two supported paths: LiveKit Cloud with the `lk` CLI, or a worker you host
yourself. Both run this project as generated.

### Option 1: LiveKit Cloud

Before the first command: authenticate once with `lk cloud auth`, then set the
project this agent belongs to as the default (`lk project list`, then
`lk project set-default "<name>"`). Without a default project every command
below fails with a subdomain mismatch that does not say which side is wrong.

The first deploy registers the agent, builds the image from the generated
`Dockerfile`, and writes the `livekit.toml` this directory deliberately does not
ship, because the platform assigns both of its values.

```sh
cp .env.example .env            # then fill in the values
{{range .Deploys}}lk agent create{{if .Region}} --region {{.Region}}{{end}}{{if .ConfigFile}} --config {{.ConfigFile}}{{end}} --secrets-file .env
{{end}}```

Every later version is a redeploy, which needs that config file and the
`Dockerfile` in this directory:

```sh
{{range .Deploys}}lk agent deploy{{if .ConfigFile}} --config {{.ConfigFile}}{{end}}
{{end}}```

`create` is the first deploy and refuses to run when a config file of that name
already exists. `deploy` ships a new version of an agent that already exists and
takes no region. Running them the wrong way round either refuses or registers a
second billable agent with dispatch split between versions.

#### Secrets

`--secrets-file .env` sends every name in `.env.example` to the platform, which
injects them as environment variables. `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, and
`LIVEKIT_API_SECRET` are supplied by the platform itself: the CLI drops them
from the file, they must not be set in the image, and their absence from the
deployed secret list is correct rather than a mistake.
{{- if .SuppliedForYou}}

`.env.example` does not name them at all, for the same reason: it lists what you
fill in, and these are not yours to fill in. On LiveKit Cloud the SIP service is
managed, so `REDIS_URL` belongs to a self-hosted deployment and is never read by
the agent. "Carrier setup" above says where each value comes from, and
`compile-report.json` carries the complete list under `required_env`.
{{- end}}

**Every name in that file must be a valid shell identifier**: letters, digits and
underscores, never starting with a digit. The platform exports your secrets with a
shell, so one bad name fails at export and that value is simply missing at
runtime. The agent then dies later, somewhere unrelated, and the only trace is a
single line near the top of `lk agent logs`:

```text
/etc/run/env: line 2: export: `11LABS_API_KEY=...': not a valid identifier
```

If you see that, rename the variable in `.env` (`ELEVENLABS_API_KEY`, not
`11LABS_API_KEY`) or delete the line, then run the overwrite command below.
Merging is not enough: the bad name stays until something replaces the whole set.

Change a value later without a full redeploy:

```sh
lk agent update-secrets --secrets-file .env              # merges into the existing set
lk agent update-secrets --secrets-file .env --overwrite  # replaces the whole set
```

#### Region
{{if .Deploys}}{{if (index .Deploys 0).Region}}
Region is chosen at the first deploy and is immutable. Moving one means creating
the agent in the new region and deleting the old one; the platform will not move
it in place.
{{- if gt (len .DeploymentRegions) 1}} Each region is its own deployment with its
own config file, and all of them keep the `{{.AgentName}}` dispatch name.
When the nearest deployment is at capacity, LiveKit may send a caller to another
region. For strict locality, use separate agent names and explicit dispatch.
Create one single-region target instance for each name.
{{- end}}
{{- else}}
No region is declared, so the first deploy asks which one to use. Set
`deployment_region` on this target in `targets.yaml` to keep the deploy
unattended. It is fixed at the first deploy and cannot be moved afterwards.
{{- end}}{{end}}

#### Check it, and recover it

```sh
lk agent status                 # Sleeping is healthy on plans that scale to zero
lk agent logs
lk agent config --id CA_xxx     # rebuild a lost config file, then deploy again
```

`unmute compile` preserves any `livekit*.toml` the platform wrote here, so a
recompile does not cost you the agent. If a config file belongs to a different
project than the current default, the deploy fails with that same subdomain
mismatch: check `lk project list` and set the default that matches the file.

### Option 2: self-hosted worker

The worker connects out to a LiveKit Server you run and needs no inbound ports.
Build the generated `Dockerfile` into an image, set `LIVEKIT_URL`,
`LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, and `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET` to your server's values plus the
provider keys from `.env.example`, then start the worker:

```sh
python -m livekit.agents start agent.py
```

See `docs-site/deploy/going-live.mdx` in the Unmute repository for the full
self-hosted setup: server, Redis, firewall ports, sizing, and scaling.
{{if or .HasWarmTransfer .HasColdTransfer}}
## Human transfers
{{if .HasWarmTransfer}}
### What the person hears on a warm transfer

The person who answers hears the handover in the **first sentence**: who is on
hold, what they want, what was already tried, then one question they can answer.
No hello, no "how can I help", and no waiting to be asked what the call is about.
When they say they can take it, the caller is put through and this agent says one
goodbye line and goes quiet.

That prompt is `_BRIEFING_PERSONA` in `agent.py`, and the comment above it says
why each paragraph is there. The prebuilt ships its own persona, but its lifecycle
lets the person speak first and never briefs unprompted, so the agent's first turn
is a reply, and a reply to "hello" is a greeting unless the prompt says otherwise.
Your package's own `briefing` text still lands last, after the caller transcript.

**One limit.** `ring_timeout` bounds **ringing only**. Once the person picks up,
nothing bounds the consultation, and the caller hears hold music for all of it.
The prompt tells this agent to decline on the person's behalf when they say they
cannot take it, go quiet, or never decide, which is a mitigation and not a
guarantee. If you see a caller held for minutes with no third log line, that is
this limit, not a bug in the dial.
{{end}}
### Reading a transfer in the logs

`lk agent logs` shows three `info` lines per transfer.
{{if .HasWarmTransfer}}
Warm:

```text
human transfer fired: <control name> (warm)
warm transfer dialling out: handing over <n> conversation messages
warm transfer merged after <n>s: <participant identity>
```

The third line is `warm transfer unavailable after <n>s: <reason>` instead for
every way the transfer did not happen: no answer, declined, voicemail, failed
dial. Exactly one of the two appears, and line 2 with no line 3 means the
consultation is still running or never ended.

The message count is the number worth reading. **Zero or one** means the briefing
had nothing to work with, which is a different problem from a briefing that was
ignored. A healthy count means material was handed over, not that the model used
it.
{{end}}
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}}
Cold:

```text
human transfer fired: <control name> (cold)
cold transfer referring the caller out
cold transfer completed after <n>s
```

or `cold transfer failed after <n>s: <reason>`. There is a fourth line, and it is
the one most people meet first:

```text
cold transfer skipped: no phone caller in the room
```

**Cold cannot be tested from the Agent Console.** It refers the caller's *existing*
SIP leg out, and a console session has no SIP leg, so there is nothing to act on.
Warm works from the console because it dials out instead. The other cause of this
line is a dispatch rule pointing at a different agent name, so the call never
reaches this worker: compare `lk sip dispatch list` with the `agentName` in the
`sip-dispatch-rule.json` in this directory.
{{end}}
No log line carries a destination, a credential, an environment variable value,
or the caller's words. The control name on the first line already says which
destination fired.
{{end}}{{if eq .TracingProvider "coval"}}
## Trace with Coval

Tracing is configured. Set `COVAL_API_KEY` in `.env`. Spans go to
`https://api.coval.dev/v1/traces` under the service name
`{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.Project}}`.

Every trace belongs to one Coval simulation, and Coval puts that simulation ID on
the call. The agent looks for it in three places:

1. **The SIP participant's attributes**, for an inbound phone call. Coval sends
   the SIP header `X-Coval-Simulation-Id`, and LiveKit only surfaces a header it
   was told about in advance. `sip-inbound-trunk.json` already carries that
   mapping, in its `headers_to_attributes` field:

   ```json
   "headers_to_attributes": {
     "X-Coval-Simulation-Id": "coval.simulation_id"
   }
   ```

   Register the trunk with `telephony-setup.sh` as usual and the attribute
   `coval.simulation_id` appears on the caller. These attributes arrive after the
   job starts, so the agent keeps watching for them rather than reading once.
2. **The agent's dispatch metadata**, for a browser or app call. See the token
   server note below.
3. **`COVAL_SIMULATION_ID`** in the environment, for a local run.

In the Coval agent config the placeholder is
`{{"{{"}}simulation_output_id{{"}}"}}`.

### What your token server has to forward

This project does not generate the token server for a deployed LiveKit agent.
On LiveKit Cloud that server is yours, and Coval calls it before it joins the
room. Two things have to line up:

- In Coval, set the LiveKit agent's **Custom Headers (JSON)** to
  `{"X-Coval-Simulation-Id": "{{"{{"}}simulation_output_id{{"}}"}}"}`.
- In your token server, read that header and put it into the room's agent
  dispatch metadata as JSON `{"coval.simulation_id": "<the header value>"}`.
  With the LiveKit server SDK that is the `metadata` argument on
  `RoomAgentDispatch`, alongside `agent_name`.

The agent reads it back as `ctx.job.metadata`. Forward nothing and the phone
route still works, because that one uses the SIP attribute instead.

`unmute dev` already does this for you locally: its `/api/session` endpoint reads
the same header and forwards it the same way.

Calls that no simulation placed are traced too. When such a call ends, the agent
submits what was said to Coval as a conversation and exports the same spans
against the conversation ID that comes back, so a local `unmute dev` run or a
real customer call lands in Trace Search. Coval needs the call to exist before
spans can attach to it, which is why this happens at the end. Nothing extra is
recorded for it: the transcript is what the trace already holds.

Until either ID is known the agent holds its spans in memory rather than sending
them. The hold is capped, and once the cap is reached the oldest spans are
dropped with a warning in the log. If `COVAL_API_KEY` is unset the agent logs a
warning and runs without tracing; an evaluation credential never takes down a
live call. A call where nobody said anything is not submitted either, because an
empty conversation is not worth an entry.

The trace is built to Coval's own shape, one `conversation` holding one `turn`
per exchange:

```
conversation                    call totals: turn counts, tool counts, duration
└── turn                        one exchange, with both transcripts on it
    ├── stt                     one per transcript, with stt.confidence
    │   └── stt.provider.<name> the transcriber that answered
    ├── vad                     the end-of-turn decision
    ├── llm                     the prompt it ran on, ttfb, tokens, finish_reason
    │   └── llm_tool_call       function.name, arguments, result, tool.error
    └── tts                     metrics.ttfb, the text that was spoken
```

Every number on those spans is LiveKit's own measurement, taken from the session
events that report it: `metrics_collected` for each model call's latency and
tokens, `conversation_item_added` for each turn's text and its transcription and
end-to-end delays, and `function_tools_executed` for every tool with its result.

A turn starts when the caller starts speaking and ends when its last piece of
work ends; the silence before the next utterance belongs to no exchange. The
`stt` span covers the caller's speech through to the transcript landing. With
preemptive generation the model starts while the caller is still speaking, so
an `llm` span can overlap the `stt` beside it, and its metric can even arrive
before LiveKit commits the utterance it answered. The round still lands in the
turn whose utterance it answered rather than in the one that happened to be
open when the metric arrived.

Each `llm` span carries the prompt that round actually ran on:
`gen_ai.system_instructions` is the system prompt of the agent holding the floor,
`input` is its message history as JSON, `tools` and `tool_count` are what it was
offered, and `agent.label` says which agent that was. One exchange can take
several rounds, through tools or a handoff, and only the round that produced
the spoken reply carries it as `output`. Those names are Pipecat's own, used on both targets on purpose: a package
compiles to both, so one vocabulary means a Coval trace metric or judge prompt
written once reads either. With handoffs that matters more than
usual, because the prompt changes under the caller mid-call and a transcript
cannot show which one produced a given answer. The history is snapshotted when
the round's metrics arrive, which is where it is right: LiveKit appends the reply
and any tool results after the request that produced them has finished. A
workflow task group is the one agent that keeps no prompt of its own, so its
instructions are read from the newest `agent_config_update` in its context,
which is the prompt actually in force.

The prompt has its own size budget, spent on the newest messages. A chat context
grows all call long and rides along on every model call, so `input` holds as
much of the tail as fits and `prompt.message_count` says how many messages there
were against `prompt.messages_traced` for how many fit.

One turn is one exchange, not one transcript. LiveKit commits a transcript each
time the caller pauses, so a single spoken sentence can arrive in several
pieces; they stay in the same turn, each with its own `stt` span, until the
agent has actually answered. `turn.user_transcript` holds them joined back
together.

LiveKit's own OpenTelemetry spans are deliberately left switched off, so
`livekit.agents.telemetry.set_tracer_provider` is never called. They are shaped
for LiveKit rather than for Coval: `user_turn` and `agent_turn` are siblings, so
the caller's speech can never sit inside the reply it caused, one exchange opens
a fresh `agent_turn` for every tool round, and about a hundred internal spans per
call arrive with names Coval has no meaning for. A span's parent is fixed when it
starts, so renaming them cannot fix the shape.

A number that was never measured is left off rather than sent as zero. A
streaming transcriber reports a transcription delay of exactly zero because there
is no per-request wait, and writing that to `metrics.ttfb` would fill Coval's
TTFB metric with zeros instead of leaving it empty. The raw figure is still there
as `stt.transcription_delay`. The same goes for token usage:
a round LiveKit reported no usage for, which happens while it swaps agents,
carries no `gen_ai.usage.*` at all rather than four zeros that would read as a
free request.

Transcripts, tool arguments and tool results are cut to `MAX_TEXT_CHARS` before
they go out, so one long tool result cannot push a batch past what Coval's ingest
accepts.

Which route correlated the trace is recorded as `coval.correlation.method` on
the `conversation` span: the delivery route when a simulation claimed the call,
with a `simulation_id_received` event beside it, or `conversation_submit` with
`coval.conversation_id` when the call was registered as a conversation instead.
Read that first if a trace lands somewhere unexpected.

These attributes are what Coval trace metrics read, so the useful metrics need
no extra setup: percentiles over `metrics.ttfb` on `llm`, `tts` and `vad` spans
show where the time goes, averages over `gen_ai.usage.*` on `llm` spans show
what the calls cost, an error rate over `llm_tool_call` spans shows whether
tools work, and the `conversation` root carries the per-call totals
(`call.duration_seconds`, `transcript.turn.count`, `tool.call.count`,
`tool.failure.count`). An LLM-judged metric created with traces included can
also read each round's real prompt and every tool result, so a judge can check
what the agent said against what its tools returned.

Starting the worker or exporting a synthetic span proves connectivity only.
Complete at least one user turn before reviewing the trace.

Traces can contain caller speech, model input and output, and tool arguments and results.
Use only fake identities and fake customer data for release tests, and do not
send real customer data until your Coval project's access and retention rules
are approved.
{{else if .Tracing}}
## Trace with Langfuse

Tracing is configured. Set `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`, and
`LANGFUSE_BASE_URL` in `.env`; all three are required. The trace name is
`{{.EntryAgent}}-{{.Project}}`; the LiveKit room name becomes the Langfuse
session ID.

Starting the worker or exporting a synthetic span proves connectivity only.
Complete at least one user turn before reviewing the trace. A traced request
contains `llm_node` and `llm_request` generation observations in addition to
the session and agent spans.

Traces can contain caller speech, model input and output, and tool arguments and results.
Use only fake identities and fake customer data for release tests. Use a separate
Langfuse project for those tests, and do not send real customer data until its
access and retention rules are approved.
{{end -}}
{{if .Telephony}}
{{- if eq .Telephony.Transport "connector"}}
## Configure the LiveKit Twilio connector

This project uses the `livekit/connector/{{.Telephony.Carrier}}` route: the
generated `telephony_bridge.py` speaks Twilio Media Streams over a WebSocket
and joins a local LiveKit room, where the agent worker handles the call. There
is no SIP trunk and no Redis. A normal HTTPS tunnel is enough, because Twilio
only needs to reach the bridge over HTTPS and WSS. This is our own open-source
bridge; LiveKit's hosted connector is Cloud only.

### Required configuration and credentials

Set these in `.env` or your deployment secret store:

- `{{.Telephony.AccountSIDEnv}}` and `{{.Telephony.AuthTokenEnv}}`: your Twilio
  Account SID and Auth Token (Twilio Console account dashboard). The Auth Token
  also validates inbound webhook signatures.
- `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`: your Voice-capable Twilio number, E.164.
- `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`: your self-hosted
  LiveKit Server. The bridge and worker connect out to it, so it needs no
  public SIP or RTP. Local `unmute dev --telephony` supplies its own dev pair.
- `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL`: the public HTTPS origin the carrier reaches; local
  `unmute dev --telephony` supplies a managed tunnel.
{{- if .Telephony.HasOutbound}}
- `UNMUTE_OUTBOUND_TOKEN`: the bearer token that authorizes
  `POST /telephony/outbound`; local `unmute dev --telephony` mints it.
{{- end}}

### Run it

Point the Twilio number's voice webhook at `POST /telephony/inbound` (the dev
command sets it automatically and prints the previous value).
{{- if .Telephony.HasOutbound}} Place an outbound call with
`unmute dev --telephony --to <E.164>`, or POST `{"to": "<E.164>"}` with the
bearer token to `/telephony/outbound` from your own application.
{{- end}}

### Scale the route

The bridge and worker are stateless: run more replicas behind one number. Each
call is one LiveKit room, and the join token dispatches the worker into it.
{{- else}}
## Telephony setup

Your carrier's SIP trunk carries the phone calls and LiveKit runs this agent, on
the `livekit/sip/{{.Telephony.Carrier}}` route. Setting it up is {{if .HasColdTransfer}}six{{else}}five{{end}} actions in
your carrier's account{{if .Telephony.HasInbound}}, then one command here{{end}}. The carrier part is yours to do:
nobody but you can sign in to your carrier account, so every step is written out
below.

You need two tools: `lk`, the LiveKit CLI, authenticated against this project
(`lk cloud auth`), and `jq`. Nothing else.

### Required configuration and credentials

Set these values in `.env` or your deployment secret store:

- `{{.Telephony.SIPAddressEnv}}`, `{{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}`, and
  `{{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}`: the address your carrier's trunk accepts calls
  on, and the username and password it accepts them with, from
  {{.Telephony.CredentialHint}}. The termination step below is where you get all
  three.
- `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`: the phone number linked to that trunk, in
  E.164 form like `+15551234567`.
- `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, and `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`: your LiveKit
  connection. On LiveKit Cloud the platform injects these into the deployed
  agent; set them yourself for a local run or a self-hosted server.
- `REDIS_URL`: the Redis that LiveKit Server and LiveKit SIP share. LiveKit
  Cloud runs its own and this agent never reads it; a self-hosted deployment
  needs one, and the generated Compose file starts one for local runs.

A normal HTTPS tunnel doesn't expose SIP signaling or RTP. Generated local
Compose defaults to SIP port `5060` and UDP RTP ports `10000-10100`; set
`LIVEKIT_SIP_HOST_PORT` and `LIVEKIT_RTP_HOST_PORT_RANGE` when those host
ports are unavailable. Set `LIVEKIT_HOST_PORT` to move the host-side LiveKit
API port from `7880`.

`unmute dev --telephony` proves the local trunk, dispatch, and worker wiring.
A real inbound call still needs publicly reachable SIP signaling and RTP ingress;
a laptop behind normal NAT does not provide that ingress.

### Get your origination URI

Both parts below need one value: the SIP address your carrier hands incoming calls
to. On LiveKit Cloud it is built from your **project ID**, not from your project
URL: drop the `p_` prefix from the ID and put the rest in front of
`.sip.livekit.cloud`. The two subdomains are different strings, so guessing from
`LIVEKIT_URL` gives an address that rings nowhere.

Run this to print every project you have configured, each with its SIP URI:

```sh
lk project list --json |
  jq -r '.[] | "\(.Name)  \(.URL)  ->  sip:\(.ProjectId | sub("^p_";"")).sip.livekit.cloud;transport=tcp"'
```

Take the line for the project this agent deploys to, and use the third column.
`lk project list` on its own marks the default project with `*`. Look at how
little the URL column and the SIP column have in common: that is why the SIP
address cannot be guessed from `LIVEKIT_URL`.

Keep that output piped through `jq`. The raw `--json` listing also contains your
project API key and secret, so it is not something to paste anywhere.

To use it in the commands below, set it once by project name:

```sh
LK_PROJECT="<the name from that list>"
LK_SIP_URI=$(lk project list --json | jq -r --arg n "$LK_PROJECT" \
  '.[] | select(.Name==$n) | "sip:\(.ProjectId | sub("^p_";"")).sip.livekit.cloud;transport=tcp"' | head -1)
echo "$LK_SIP_URI"
```

*Self-hosted LiveKit:* there is no project SIP URI. The address is the public SIP
signaling endpoint of the LiveKit SIP service you deployed.

{{if eq .Telephony.Carrier "twilio"}}
### At your carrier (Twilio)

Do these in the [Twilio Console](https://console.twilio.com), in this order. Each
one names the console path, and the CLI equivalent where Twilio has one.

1. **Get a voice-capable phone number.** Phone Numbers, Manage, Buy a number,
   with the Voice capability. Skip this if you already own the number you want
   this agent to use.
2. **Create an Elastic SIP trunk.** Elastic SIP Trunking, Manage, Trunks, Create
   new SIP trunk. One trunk carries everything below.
3. **Termination, which lets this agent place calls.** On the trunk's Termination
   tab, set the Termination SIP URI to a domain name you choose ending in
   `pstn.twilio.com`, for example `my-agent.pstn.twilio.com`. That one domain is
   the value of `{{.Telephony.SIPAddressEnv}}`; there is no second address to
   look for. Then under Authentication, select or create a Credential List: the
   username and password you put in it are the values of
   `{{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}` and `{{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}`.
4. **Origination, which lets calls reach this agent.**{{if not .Telephony.HasInbound}} Only needed if you want
   this number to receive calls; this package only places them.{{end}} On the
   trunk's Origination tab, add an Origination URI: your LiveKit project SIP URI
   with `;transport=tcp` on the end, like
   `sip:abc123def.sip.livekit.cloud;transport=tcp`. Leave priority and weight at
   1 and tick Enabled. Two ways to get that URI: read it from the LiveKit Cloud
   project settings page, or derive it from the project ID with the command in
   **Get your origination URI** below. **Do not build it from `LIVEKIT_URL`**:
   the project URL subdomain and the SIP subdomain are different strings.

   *Self-hosted LiveKit:* there is no project SIP URI. Point origination at the
   public SIP signaling address of the LiveKit SIP service you deployed instead.
   Every other step on this page is the same.
5. **Attach the number to the trunk.** The trunk's Numbers tab, Add an existing
   number. The number you attach is the value of
   `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`. CLI: `twilio api trunking v1 trunks
   phone-numbers create --trunk-sid <trunk-sid> --phone-number-sid <number-sid>`.
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}}
6. **Turn transfer on, because this agent hands calls to a person.** On the same
   trunk, under Features: set Call Transfer (SIP REFER) to Enabled,
   tick Enable PSTN Transfer, and choose a Caller ID for Transfer Target. CLI:
   `twilio api trunking v1 trunks update --sid <trunk-sid> --transfer-mode
   enable-all --transfer-caller-id from-transferee`. Transfer caller ID is a
   trunk setting, never something one call can choose.
{{- end}}

LiveKit's own version of these steps is the
[{{.Telephony.Carrier}} provider guide]({{.Telephony.ProviderDocs}}), if you want
a second description of the same thing.

#### The same steps as commands

Prerequisites: the [Twilio CLI](https://www.twilio.com/docs/twilio-cli/getting-started/install)
with a profile (`twilio login`), plus `lk` and `jq`. Run the block from this
directory, top to bottom, with `LK_SIP_URI` already set above. Nothing is
copied by hand:
each command finds what it needs from the one before it, by the domain and number
you already know. Verified against `twilio-cli` 6.2.4 and `lk` 2.18.2 on
2026-08-12.

```sh
# You choose these two. The domain must be unique across Twilio and end in
# pstn.twilio.com. Twilio requires the password to be 12 characters or longer,
# mixed case, with at least one digit.
TRUNK_DOMAIN="{{.Project}}-trunk.pstn.twilio.com"
SIP_USERNAME="{{.Project}}-agent"
read -rsp "SIP password: " SIP_PASSWORD; echo

# The number you already own, read from .env exactly as telephony-setup.sh reads it.
NUMBER=$(sed -n 's/^{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}=//p' ./.env | head -1 | tr -d "\r\"'")

# Steps 1 and 2: the trunk, then its SID looked up by the domain you just chose.
# Already have a trunk? Skip the create and list what you have instead:
#   twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:list -o json |
#     jq -r '.[] | "\(.friendlyName)  \(.domainName)  \(.sid)"'
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:create --friendly-name "{{.Project}}" --domain-name "$TRUNK_DOMAIN"
TRUNK_SID=$(twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:list -o json |
  jq -r --arg d "$TRUNK_DOMAIN" '.[] | select(.domainName==$d) | .sid')

# Step 3, termination: the trunk domain is the address, and a credential list
# holds the username and password. Three commands: create the list, put the
# credential in it, attach it to the trunk.
twilio api:core:sip:credential-lists:create --friendly-name "{{.Project}}"
CL_SID=$(twilio api:core:sip:credential-lists:list -o json |
  jq -r --arg n "{{.Project}}" '.[] | select(.friendlyName==$n) | .sid' | head -1)
twilio api:core:sip:credential-lists:credentials:create --credential-list-sid "$CL_SID" \
  --username "$SIP_USERNAME" --password "$SIP_PASSWORD"
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:credential-lists:create --trunk-sid "$TRUNK_SID" \
  --credential-list-sid "$CL_SID"

# Step 4, origination: where Twilio sends calls that arrive on your number.
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:origination-urls:create --trunk-sid "$TRUNK_SID" \
  --friendly-name "LiveKit SIP" --sip-url "$LK_SIP_URI" --weight 1 --priority 1 --enabled

# Step 5: attach the number to the trunk.
NUMBER_SID=$(twilio phone-numbers:list -o json |
  jq -r --arg n "$NUMBER" '.[] | select(.phoneNumber==$n) | .sid')
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:phone-numbers:create --trunk-sid "$TRUNK_SID" \
  --phone-number-sid "$NUMBER_SID"
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}}

# Step 6: allow transfers, including out to the phone network. Without this the
# carrier rejects the cold transfer.
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:update --sid "$TRUNK_SID" \
  --transfer-mode enable-all --transfer-caller-id from-transferee
{{- end}}

# Finally, put the three termination values in .env:
#   {{.Telephony.SIPAddressEnv}}=$TRUNK_DOMAIN
#   {{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}=$SIP_USERNAME
#   {{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}=the password you typed above
```

#### Check the carrier side

```sh
twilio phone-numbers:list -o json |
  jq -r --arg n "$NUMBER" '.[] | select(.phoneNumber==$n) | "trunk: \(.trunkSid // "none")"'
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:list -o json |
  jq -r --arg s "$TRUNK_SID" '.[] | select(.sid==$s) | "transfers: \(.transferMode), caller id: \(.transferCallerId)"'
twilio api:trunking:v1:trunks:origination-urls:list --trunk-sid "$TRUNK_SID" -o json |
  jq -r '.[] | "origination: \(.sipUrl) enabled=\(.enabled)"'
```

Expect the trunk SID you just created, `transfers: enable-all`, and your LiveKit
SIP URI enabled. These three lines are the three ways this side goes wrong:
`trunk: none` means calls to that number never reach LiveKit;
`transfers: disable-all` or `sip-only` is what makes a cold transfer to a phone
number fail; a missing or disabled origination URI means the call arrives at
Twilio and stops there.
{{else}}
### At your carrier

Your carrier's console names these differently, so here is what has to be true
rather than where to click. The
[{{.Telephony.Carrier}} provider guide]({{.Telephony.ProviderDocs}}) has that
carrier's own paths.

1. A voice-capable phone number you own.
2. A SIP trunk on your carrier account. One trunk carries everything below.
3. A termination address, which is where this agent sends the calls it places,
   with username and password authentication. Those three values are
   `{{.Telephony.SIPAddressEnv}}`, `{{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}`, and
   `{{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}`.
4. An origination target, which is where the carrier sends incoming calls: your
   LiveKit project SIP URI with `;transport=tcp` on the end, from **Get your
   origination URI** below.{{if not .Telephony.HasInbound}} Only needed if you want this number to
   receive calls; this package only places them.{{end}}

   *Self-hosted LiveKit:* there is no project SIP URI. Point origination at the
   public SIP signaling address of the LiveKit SIP service you deployed instead.
5. That phone number attached to that trunk. Its value is
   `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`.
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}}
6. Transfer enabled on the trunk, including transfers out to the phone network,
   because this agent hands callers to a person.
{{- end}}
{{end}}
{{- if .Telephony.HasInbound}}
### At LiveKit

One command, run from this directory with `.env` filled in:

```sh
bash telephony-setup.sh
```

It creates the two records an incoming call needs, and it finds them by your
phone number, so there is no ID to copy anywhere:

- the inbound trunk, which claims your number for this LiveKit project;
- the dispatch rule, which routes calls on that number to this agent, one
  `call-*` room per caller.

Only incoming calls need records like these. A call arrives with no request of
yours for configuration to travel with, so the platform has to already know
which project owns the number and where the caller goes. Placing a call is the
opposite: your own code starts it, so the trunk settings ride along.

Run the command again any time. Whatever already exists is reused, and it says
which. Two failures it can report: your number is already claimed by a trunk
this project cannot see, which means someone else owns it; or `lk` is not
authenticated, which `lk cloud auth` fixes.

Earlier builds of this project also needed `LIVEKIT_SIP_INBOUND_TRUNK` in the
environment. That variable is retired and nothing reads it, so delete it from
`.env` and from your platform secrets.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Telephony.HasInbound}}

### Take it live, in order

The two halves above can be done in either order, but a call only works once both
exist and the agent is running. This is the whole sequence, from a compiled build
directory:

1. **Carrier**: the steps above. If you already have a trunk, the two that are
   easy to miss are attaching your number to it and, for a cold transfer, the
   transfer toggles. A number that is not on the trunk never reaches LiveKit, no
   matter how right the origination URI is.
2. **LiveKit records**: `bash telephony-setup.sh`, then run it again and expect
   every line to say reused.
3. **The agent**: `lk agent deploy` from this directory (or `lk agent create` the
   first time, see Deploy above), then `lk agent update-secrets --secrets-file .env
   --overwrite` if you changed `.env`. Check `lk agent status`.
4. **Call your number** from a phone. The agent answers.
{{- if .HasColdTransfer}} Ask for whatever the cold
   transfer covers; you should be connected onward and the agent should drop out.
{{- end}}
5. **Read `lk agent logs`.** The transfer lines are listed under Human transfers
   below. Check the top of the log too: that is where a bad secret name shows up.

If the call rings and drops with no agent, work backwards: does
`lk sip inbound list` show a trunk claiming your number, does `lk sip dispatch list`
show a rule for it naming `{{.AgentName}}`, and does `lk agent status` show the
agent up. Each of those is one of the three steps above.
{{- end}}
{{- if or .HasColdTransfer .HasWarmTransfer}}

### What transfers need
{{if .HasColdTransfer}}
Cold transfer needs nothing at LiveKit beyond the records above. It refers the
caller's existing call out through your carrier, so the carrier toggles in
step 6 are its only setting. It cannot be tested from the Agent Console, because
a console session has no phone leg to refer. When the logs say
`cold transfer failed after <n>s`, the usual cause is that step 6 was skipped:
Call Transfer (SIP REFER) or PSTN transfer is off on the trunk.
{{end}}{{if .HasWarmTransfer}}
Warm transfer needs nothing at LiveKit either. It places a second call with the
four carrier values above, so termination is what it depends on.
{{end}}{{- end}}
{{- if or .Telephony.HasOutbound .Telephony.HasWarm}}
### How dialling out works

Those four carrier values are all this agent needs to dial out. It passes them
inline with every call, so **no LiveKit outbound trunk is registered and none is
read**. If you set up an earlier build of this project, `lk sip outbound create`
and `LIVEKIT_SIP_OUTBOUND_TRUNK` are no longer part of it: delete that variable,
and delete the trunk itself with `lk sip outbound delete` when you like. Setting
it changes nothing, because nothing reads it.

What a stored trunk did carry and inline configuration does not: a list of
caller-ID numbers the platform picks from per call, trunk metadata copied onto
every SIP participant, and one place to change the host without touching a
deployment. If you need one of those, the platform feature still exists; it is
just not something this project emits.
{{- end}}
{{if .Telephony.HasOutbound}}
### Place an outbound call

Dispatch this agent to a new room with JSON job metadata in this shape:

```json
{
  "direction": "outbound",
  "phone_number": "+14155550123",
  "call_start": {}
}
```

The worker validates the E.164 destination and authored `call_start` fields
before dialing, then dials with the carrier's trunk settings inline. Nothing
has to be registered on the platform first.
{{end}}

### Scale the route

Redis is control-plane infrastructure, not an audio hop. LiveKit Server uses
it for distributed room state and routing; LiveKit SIP uses the same Redis
deployment to coordinate SIP participants with the server. Audio still flows
through the SIP/RTP and LiveKit media services. Scale worker replicas on active
jobs, keep the shared Redis available, and drain active calls before shutdown.
{{- end}}
{{end -}}

